\section{Discussion \& Methodological Anatomy}

\subsection{The Anatomy of the PANDA Collapse}
In Table~\ref{tab:results_matrix}, the most substantial discrepancy occurs on the PANDA prostate biopsy benchmark. The original authors reported $88.0\%$ accuracy on 6-class ISUP grading alongside a $99.0\%$ Dice score. In contrast, across all 12 PANDA configurations tested, empirical top-1 accuracy spans $30.89\%-46.15\%$, with standard baselines peaking at $46.15\%$ Acc / $44.34\%$ Dice (Run 04).

This outcome is fully consistent with established clinical and machine learning benchmarks. In the international PANDA challenge ($>1,000$ participating teams)~\cite{bulten2022artificial}, top-performing multi-instance learning (MIL) algorithms on whole-slide images achieved quadratic weighted kappa scores of $0.86-0.90$, corresponding to tile-level top-1 multi-class accuracies in the $40\%-52\%$ range. Human pathologists achieve inter-observer agreement kappa of only $0.65-0.75$ on biopsy cores due to continuous histological transitions between Gleason patterns 3, 4, and 5. Claiming $88\%$ accuracy on $128 \times 128$ isolated patches with a basic 2D CNN is unfeasible without patient-level data leakage (where tiles from the same biopsy core contaminate both train and test splits).

\subsection{Controlled Skip Connection Ablation}
To evaluate the structural necessity of skip connections, we analyze strictly matched configurations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Macro-tumor structures (TCGA):} Comparing the skip-ablated MobileNetV2 (Run 25) against its matched baseline (Run 08, both at $\eta=10^{-4}$, GradNorm $\alpha=1.5$, Macenko normalized) reveals that accuracy shifts from $93.32\%$ to $94.34\%$ ($+1.02\%$) while Dice remains essentially constant at $84.12\%$ vs $84.20\%$ ($-0.08\%$). Coarse macro-tumor segmentation relies primarily on high-level semantic context adequately preserved in the bottleneck.
    \item \textbf{Micro-glandular structures (PANDA):} Comparing the matched MobileNetV2 configurations (Run 10 baseline at $34.70\%$ Acc / $31.34\%$ Dice vs Run 26 skip-ablated at $35.31\%$ Acc / $28.94\%$ Dice) demonstrates that removing skip connections degrades fine boundary delineation ($\Delta_{\text{Dice}} = -2.40\%$), while top-1 classification accuracy remains bottlenecked by the shared representation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dissecting Dynamic Gradient Balancing vs. Learning Rate}
In Section~3, Group 4 isolates the effects of GradNorm and learning rate on PANDA $\times$ VGG16:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{At $\eta=10^{-3}$:} Static 5:1 weighting (Run 20) achieves $45.39\%$ Acc / $44.08\%$ Dice. Introducing GradNorm ($\alpha=1.5$, Run 18) yields $42.54\%$ Acc / $40.84\%$ Dice, isolating a modest initial penalty on the classification head ($\Delta_{\text{Acc}} = -2.85\%$).
    \item \textbf{At $\eta=10^{-4}$:} Static weighting (Run 17) yields $43.35\%$ Acc / $37.99\%$ Dice. However, combining $\eta=10^{-4}$ with GradNorm ($\alpha=1.5$, Run 09) causes severe degradation to $30.89\%$ Acc / $31.59\%$ Dice ($\Delta_{\text{Acc}} = -12.46\%$).
\end{itemize}
Critically, GradNorm incurs only a modest penalty at $\eta=10^{-3}$ ($\Delta_{\text{Acc}} = -2.85\%$), but exacerbates performance collapse at $\eta=10^{-4}$ ($\Delta_{\text{Acc}} = -12.46\%$), demonstrating that learning rate scale and dynamic gradient asymmetry compound multiplicatively rather than acting independently. For highly asymmetric multi-task objectives (such as 6-class fine-grained grading coupled with dense segmentation), an aggressive asymmetry parameter ($\alpha=1.5$) heavily suppresses the classification head's gradient updates. For such tasks, dynamic gradient balancing should either utilize milder balancing coefficients ($\alpha \le 0.5$) or decoupled, task-specific projection heads.

\section{Conclusion \& Recommendations}
Through an exhaustive 26-experiment campaign, we demonstrated that hard-parameter-sharing U-Nets cannot achieve the inflated $88\%-99\%$ metrics reported in recent literature for fine-grained multi-task pathology. We trace these claims to background-Dice evaluation artifacts and patient data leakage. For future computational pathology studies, we recommend: (1) mandatory patient/biopsy-level \texttt{GroupKFold} splitting, (2) foreground-only or zero-penalty Dice reporting, and (3) validating multi-task backbones on diverse multi-organ benchmarks such as PanNuke.

